perm_action @a (t:wire mut) () () () ()
regs {  } bounds {2}
^entry():
qloop 2 {
b = qborrow any 1
x b
qreturn b
}
Its interface/closure H is 3bc76b4398502416eed66400092c8ec2e5e273d97283b5199065a88a3ed9f1d7; with that stored symbol hash its object_hash is 9b10654c083129047b847830d3582ff3dbf15cf7c8759f4586f836c02e5b6266. Without a certificate, check correctly exits 1 with §5.6: qreturn does not restore borrowed wire v1_0. Append a correctly bound certificate region for H with this single claim:
{"kind":"exhaustive","label":"proof","domain":{"bundles":[{"name":"t","width":1,"lo":0,"hi":1}],"borrowed":[{"name":"b","width":1,"lo":0,"hi":1}],"regions":[]},"expected":{"table":[[0,0],[0,1],[1,0],[1,1]]}}
and verification.borrow_frames=[0]. Use the usual valid empty resources/gates/cleanup/error/composition records, the subject signature/bounds, frame.interface=[{"role":"mut","width":1,"class":"any"}], frame.borrow={"any":1}, and bindings object_hash as above, interface_hash=closure_hash=reference_program_hash=H, dialect_mapping_version="test.1", target_hash=64 copies of "3". Reproduced: check now exits 0/status ok; run-action a --mut 0 returns identity, and reverse reports equals_entry=true. validate_borrow_frames suppresses the actual Return failure because the fallback re-executes only the complete region on one reused pool bit. Hand: at EACH return beta has become beta XOR 1, so the returned lifetime is invalid irrespective of a later borrowing. Also reproduced the muse variant replacing x b by cx b t: H=1e0d1484447af491bd512ef5cc3cbe4f1b9532863aa56ae2149f61247973f343, object_hash=4aaf72716633ad6037e3b542f000eb31372accd743ddb97aa97d2960447ac43b. The same identity claim makes it pass, although each lifetime makes t depend on its independent beta_i. These are the old round-4 failures reachable through an alternate admission path.

